    <br />
    <hr size="1" />
    <div id="sC-component-footnotes" class="ts-footnote-2">
<p><a name="footnote1"></a><sup>1</sup> I would like to thank Marcus  Petersen, Jacqueline Yen, and Patricia Zobel for their assistance in preparing  these remarks, Ashley Rhodes for her assistance with data, and colleagues in  the Federal Reserve System for valuable comments and suggestions. </p>
<p><a name="footnote2"></a><sup>2</sup> See <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20190130c.htm" target="_blank">Statement  Regarding Monetary Policy Implementation and Balance Sheet Normalization</a>,  Federal Reserve Board of Governors, January 30, 2019.</p>
<p><a name="footnote3"></a><sup>3</sup> The Federal Reserve Banks pay  interest on required reserve balances (IORR) and on excess reserve balances  (IOER) under authority in Section 19 of the Federal Reserve Act. The Board of  Governors has prescribed rules governing the payment of interest by Federal  Reserve Banks in Regulation D. Since 2008, these two rates have been set at the  same value.</p>
<p><a name="footnote4"></a><sup>4</sup> For a review of these frameworks  please see <a href="http://dx.doi.org/10.17016/FEDS.2015.047" target="_blank">Monetary Policy 101: A Primer on the Fed&rsquo;s Changing Approach to  Policy Implementation</a>, Jane E. Ihrig, Ellen E. Meade, and Gretchen C. Weinbach  (2015). Finance and Economics Discussion Series 2015-047. Washington: Board of  Governors of the Federal Reserve System.</p>
<p><a name="footnote5"></a><sup>5</sup> See <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomcminutes20181219.htm" target="_blank">Minutes  of the Federal Open Market Committee</a>, December 18-19, 2018, and <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomcminutes20190130.htm" target="_blank">Minutes  of the Federal Open Market Committee</a>, January 29-30, 2019.</p>
<p><a name="footnote6"></a><sup>6</sup> Simon Potter features some such  analysis in <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2017/pot170405" target="_blank">Money  Markets at a Crossroads: Policy Implementation at a Time of Structural Change</a>,  remarks at the Master of Applied Economics&rsquo; Distinguished Speaker Series,  University of California, Los Angeles, April 5, 2017.</p>
<p><a name="footnote7"></a><sup>7</sup> See <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomcminutes20181108.htm" target="_blank">Minutes  of the Federal Open Market Committee</a>, November 7-8, 2018.</p>
<p><a name="footnote8"></a><sup>8</sup> Liabilities other than reserves  have also grown significantly and have played a role in the expansion of the Federal  Reserve&rsquo;s balance sheet. The magnitude of these non-reserve liabilities as well  as the flows affecting their variability is not closely related to monetary  policy decisions. Many of the Federal Reserve&rsquo;s liabilities arise from  statutory responsibilities, such as supplying currency and serving as the U.S. Treasury&rsquo;s  fiscal agent. Each liability provides social benefits to the economy and plays  an important role as a safe and liquid asset for the public, the banking  system, the U.S. government, or other institutions. For further information,  please see the box &ldquo;The Role of Liabilities in Determining the Size of the  Federal Reserve&rsquo;s Balance Sheet&rdquo; in Part 2 of the <em><a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/2019-02-mpr-summary.htm" target="_blank">Monetary  Policy Report submitted to the Congress on  February 22, 2019, pursuant to section 2B of the Federal Reserve Act</a></em> and &ldquo;Explaining Changes in the Federal Reserve&rsquo;s Balance  Sheet&rdquo;, Box 3, <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/markets/annual_reports.html" target="_blank">Open Market  Operations During 2018 Report</a>, April 2019.</p>
<p><a name="footnote9"></a><sup>9</sup> &nbsp;See  the <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/policy-normalization.htm" target="_blank">FOMC&rsquo;s  Communications related to Policy Normalization</a>. The <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20190130c.htm" target="_blank">Statement  Regarding Monetary Policy Implementation and Balance Sheet Normalization</a> and <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20190320c.htm" target="_blank">Balance  Sheet Normalization Principles and Plans</a> released following the  January and March FOMC meetings, respectively, together revise and replace the  FOMC&rsquo;s earlier Policy Normalization Principles and Plans.  </p>
<p><a name="footnote10"></a><sup>10</sup> See Chair Powell&rsquo;s <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomcpresconf20190130.htm" target="_blank">press  conference</a> following Federal Open Market Committee, January 29-30,  2019.</p>
<p><a name="footnote11"></a><sup>11</sup> Banks are subject to reserve  requirements as set forth in Regulation D, 12 CFR 2014. Reserve requirements  are calculated as a proportion of an institution&rsquo;s transaction deposits during  a 14-day computation period. Institutions satisfy their requirement by  maintaining an average level of vault cash or balances in their account at a  Federal Reserve Bank equal to the reserve requirement over a lagged, two-week  maintenance period. The portion of the reserve requirement that is not  satisfied by vault cash is called the reserve balance requirement.</p>
<p><a name="footnote12"></a><sup>12</sup> For further information, please  see the box &ldquo;Interest on Reserves and Its Importance for Monetary Policy&rdquo; in  Part 2 of the <em><a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/2018-07-mpr-part2.htm" target="_blank">Monetary  Policy Report submitted to the Congress on July  13, 2018, pursuant to section 2B of the Federal Reserve Act</a></em>.</p>
<p><a name="footnote13"></a><sup>13</sup> See Randal K. Quarles (2019), <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/speech/quarles20190222a.htm" target="_blank">The  Future of the Federal Reserve&rsquo;s Balance Sheet</a>, remarks at the 2019  U.S. Monetary Policy Forum, New York, New York, February 22, 2019, and Randal  K. Quarles (2018), <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/speech/quarles20180504a.htm" target="_blank">Liquidity  Regulation and the Size of the Fed's Balance Sheet</a>, remarks at  &quot;Currencies, Capital, and Central Bank Balances: A Policy  Conference,&quot; a Hoover Institution Monetary Policy Conference, Stanford  University, Stanford, Calif., May 4, 2018.</p>
<p><a name="footnote14"></a><sup>14</sup> The Board of Governors, under  delegated authority from the Office of Management and Budget, is authorized to  conduct the <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/data/sfos/sfos.htm" target="_blank">Senior Financial  Officer Surveys</a>. The survey was conducted jointly with the Federal  Reserve Bank of New York in September 2018 and for the second time in February  2019. The purpose of the surveys was to gather views systematically from a  number of banks concerning their reserve balance management strategies and  practices. Survey questions asked senior financial officers from each  responding bank to identify the approximate lowest level of their institutions&rsquo;  reserve balances that they would feel comfortable holding before taking actions  to increase their reserve balance levels. The surveys also asked about the  various factors that drive this determination, how a shift in short-term  interest rates would change this level, and the likelihood of choosing  different liabilities to rebuild a reserve position. The surveys are voluntary.  All answers are used solely for internal analytical, not supervisory, purposes.</p>
<p><a name="footnote15"></a><sup>15</sup> The exact question  respondents were asked was &ldquo;Please rate the importance to your bank of the  following considerations in determining the amount you indicated in question 1  on a scale of 1 (not important), 2 (somewhat important), 3 (important), or 4  (very important), or, if the factor is not applicable to your bank (for  example, your bank is not subject to the regulatory requirement or does not  engage in the described activity), please select &lsquo;N/A.&rsquo;&rdquo; Factors included: a)  Meeting routine intraday payment flows, b) Meeting potential deposit outflows,  c) Satisfying internal liquidity stress metrics, d) Satisfying the bank&rsquo;s reserve requirements,  e) Seeking to earn the IOER rate instead of the return on other high-quality  liquid assets, f) Managing liquidity portfolio based on monetization  assumptions for high-quality liquid assets. For further information, see the <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/data/sfos/sfos.htm" target="_blank">September  2018 Senior Financial Officer Survey</a> results.</p>
<p><a name="footnote16"></a><sup>16</sup> The exact question respondents  were asked was &ldquo;Given the constellation of short-term interest rates relative to  the interest on excess reserves (IOER) rate over the past month, what is the  approximate lowest dollar level of reserve balances that your bank would be  comfortable holding before it began taking active steps to maintain or increase  its reserve balance position?&rdquo; For further information, see the <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/data/sfos/sfos.htm" target="_blank">September  2018 Senior Financial Officer Survey</a> results. </p>
<p><a name="footnote17"></a><sup>17</sup> For additional detail on how these  estimates were constructed see Thomas Keating, et al. (2019). <a href="https://doi.org/10.17016/2380-7172.2327" target="_blank">Estimating  System Demand for Reserve Balances Using the 2018 Senior Financial Officer  Survey</a>, FEDS Notes. Washington: Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve  System, April 9, 2019.</p>
<p><a name="footnote18"></a><sup>18</sup> These balance sheet costs can  manifest themselves in less federal funds activity later in the day at rates  near IOER. Prior to the crisis the federal funds market was liquid until late  in the day. Banks could experience unanticipated late day outflows and still be  confident in their ability to borrow from other banks holding excess reserves.  Today, there is little late day activity in the federal funds market. As  reserves decline and there is greater need to redistribute reserves, it is  unclear if this activity will return as the opportunity cost of holding  reserves is now lower. In the post-crisis era of super abundant reserves, bank  lenders have largely left the federal funds market and today almost all lending  is done by the Federal Home Loan Banks. In Desk outreach and the SFOS  responses, many banks have indicated that rates would need to be well above  IOER before the economics would be attractive enough to offset balance sheet  costs that lending in the federal funds market incurs. </p>
<p><a name="footnote19"></a><sup>19</sup> For an overview of bank microdata collected  by the Federal Reserve see the <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/data/mdrm.htm" target="_blank">Micro Data Reference Manual</a>.</p>
<p><a name="footnote20"></a><sup>20</sup> Borrowing in overnight unsecured  markets from public-sector entities (such as the FHLBs), foreign central banks,  and nonfinancial corporations and&mdash;all else equal&mdash;keeping those funds as  reserves in a bank&rsquo;s Federal Reserve account improves the bank&rsquo;s LCR ratio. For  a full description of the U.S. LCR, including which banks are covered, see  Regulation WW--Liquidity Risk Management Standards, 12 C.F.R. pt. 249 (2017). </p>
 <p><a name="footnote21"></a><sup>21</sup> Purchases of Treasury securities  increase the quantity of reserve balances in the banking system; sales or  maturities of Treasury securities reduce those balances. These transactions,  which are conducted by the New York Fed's Open Market Trading Desk (the Desk),  are executed with primary dealers. Repo transactions temporarily increase the  quantity of reserve balances in the banking system. In a repo transaction, the  Desk purchases Treasury, agency debt, or agency mortgage-backed securities  (MBS) from a counterparty subject to an agreement to resell the securities at a  later date. It is economically similar to a loan collateralized by securities  having a value higher than the loan to protect the Desk against market and  credit risk.  </p>
<p><a name="footnote22"></a><sup>22</sup> I discuss these issues related to  non-reserve liabilities in more detail in <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2018/log180504" target="_blank">Operational  Perspectives on Monetary Policy Implementation: Panel Remarks on &quot;The  Future of the Central Bank Balance Sheet&quot;</a> remarks at the Policy  Conference on Currencies, Capital, and Central Bank Balances, Hoover Institution,  Stanford University, Stanford, May 4, 2018 and <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2017/log170518" target="_blank">Implementing  Monetary Policy: Perspective from the Open Market Trading Desk</a>,  remarks before the Money Marketeers of New York University, New York City, May  18, 2017.</p>
<p><a name="footnote23"></a><sup>23</sup> See BIS Committee on the Global Financial System (CGFS) Paper No. 59, <a href="https://www.bis.org/publ/cgfs59.htm" target="_blank">Repo Market  Functioning</a>, April 2017.</p>
<p><a name="footnote24"></a><sup>24</sup> The relationship is not one-to-one  because typically there are other changes to the balance sheet that also impact  the level of reserves. </p>
<p><a name="footnote25"></a><sup>25</sup> See <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/markets/opolicy/operating_policy_190320" target="_blank">Statement  Regarding Reinvestment in Treasury Securities and Agency Mortgage-Backed  Securities</a>, Federal Reserve Bank of New York, March 20, 2019.</p>
<p><a name="footnote26"></a><sup>26</sup> See discussion in <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomcminutes20190320.htm" target="_blank">Minutes  of the Federal Open Market Committee</a>, March 19-20, 2019.</p>

    </div>
         

</div>

</div>
